<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Popular Front (Chile)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1936">1936</a>–<a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-france">Popular Front</a> was a coalition of Chilean centre and left parties, led by the Radical Party with the Socialists and Communists, formed in 1936. Its candidate Pedro Aguirre Cerda narrowly won the presidential election of 1938, and his government founded the development agency <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corfo">CORFO</a> in 1939 to promote industry after the Chillán earthquake. The coalition broke up in 1941.</p></div>
